<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="MT-notes">


<title>Module 5: Matrix Transformations</title>

<objectives>

<p>
After completing this module, you should be able to:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Interpret a matrix as a linear transformation from <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> to <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.</li>
<li>Produce a matrix representation of a linear transformation given its action on the standard unit vectors.</li>
<li>Identify basic geometric transformations of the plane.</li>
<li>Interpret a matrix product as a composition of linear transformations.</li>
</ul>

</objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
For more details on this material, see
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/XLAChapter_linTrans/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Linear Transformations Chapter)</url>.
</p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="linear-transformations">
<title>Linear Transformations</title>

<p>
<alert>Definition (Transformation).</alert>
A transformation (function, mapping)
<m>T:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m</m>
assigns to each
<m>x\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>
a vector
<m>T(x)\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Domain:</alert> <m>\mathbb{R}^n\quad</m>
<nbsp/>
<alert>Codomain:</alert> <m>\mathbb{R}^m\quad</m>
<nbsp/>
<alert>Range:</alert>
<m>\{T(x):x\in\mathbb{R}^n\}</m>.
</p>

<image>
<shortdescription>Diagram showing a transformation from a domain in R^n to a codomain in R^m, with the range highlighted.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!8] (-3,0) ellipse (1.6 and 1.0);
  \node at (-3,1.4) {\small Domain $\mathbb{R}^n$};
  \draw[thick] (3,0) ellipse (2.3 and 1.0);
  \node at (3,1.4) {\small Codomain $\mathbb{R}^m$};
    \draw[blue,fill=blue!5] (2.4,0) ellipse (1.45 and 0.7);
  \node at (2,0) {\small \color{blue}Range of  $T$};
  \node at (-2.85,0.7) {\small $x$};
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!50] (-2.6,0.7) circle (0.07);
  \node at (-3.15,0.15) {\small $0$};
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!50] (-3,0) circle (0.07);
  \node at (-2.85,-0.7) {\small $y$};
  \draw[thick,fill=gray!50] (-2.6,-0.7) circle (0.07);
    \node at (3.5,0.1) {\color{blue}\small $0$};
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!50] (3.3,0) circle (0.07);
  \node at (0, -1.6) {\small Range is the set of all images $T(x)$ in $\mathbb{R}^m$};
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!50] (2.1,0.5) circle (0.07);
  \draw[->] (-2.5,0.7)--(2,0.5) node[near end, above]{\small $T(x)$};
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!50] (2.1,-0.5) circle (0.07);
  \draw[->] (-2.5,-0.7)--(2,-0.5) node[near end, below]{\small $T(y)$};

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<p>
<alert>Matrix Transformations.</alert>
A transformation
<m>T:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m</m>
is a matrix transformation if there exists an
<m>m\times n</m>
matrix
<m>A</m>
such that
</p>

<me>
T(x)=Ax.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Dimension check.</alert>
Vector <m>x</m> must have <m>n</m> entries and
<m>Ax\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
</p>

<definition>
<p><alert>Definition (Linear Transformation).</alert>
Transformation <m>T</m>
is linear if
<me>
T(u+v)=T(u)+T(v),
\qquad
T(cu)=cT(u)
</me>
for all vectors
<m>u,v</m>
and scalars
<m>c</m>.</p>
</definition>

<theorem>
<p><alert>Fact.</alert>
Matrix transformations are linear (because
<m>
A(u+v)=Au+Av,
\text{ and }
A(cu)=c(Au)
</m>).</p>
</theorem>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="computing-ax">
<title>Example: Computing Ax and Interpreting Ax = b</title>

<p>
<alert>Matrix and vectors.</alert>
Let
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1\\
2 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
u=
\begin{bmatrix}
1.6\\
0.9
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
v=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Compute</alert> <m>Au</m>.
</p>

<me>
Au
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1\\
2 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1.6\\
0.9
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2.5\\
5
\end{bmatrix}
=
2.5
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
2
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Compute</alert> <m>Av</m>.
</p>

<me>
Av
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 1\\
2 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
-1
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Geometrically, all vectors in the direction of
<m>v</m>
are mapped to the zero vector.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Interpretation.</alert>
Multiplication by <m>A</m>
maps the entire plane onto the line generated by
<m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
Solving
<m>Ax=b</m>
asks whether
<m>b</m>
lies on that line.
</p>
<image>
<shortdescription>Diagram showing a matrix transformation mapping a plane onto a line.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node at (0,4) {\bf Original space $\mathbb{R}^2$};
\draw[->,black] (-3,0) -- (3,0);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,1) -- (3,1);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,2) -- (3,2);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,3) -- (3,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,-1) -- (3,-1);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,-2) -- (3,-2);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,-3) -- (3,-3);
\draw[->,black] (0,-3) -- (0,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (1,-3) -- (1,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (2,-3) -- (2,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (3,-3) -- (3,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-1,-3) -- (-1,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-2,-3) -- (-2,3);
\draw[-,gray!50] (-3,-3) -- (-3,3);
\node at (-0.1,0.2) {\small $0$};
\node at (0.6,0.6) {\small $u$};
\node at (0.5,-0.7) {\small $v$};
\draw[->,blue,thick] (0,0)  -- (1.69,0.9);
\draw[->,blue,thick] (0,0)  -- (1,-1);

  \draw[->, very thick] (3.5,0) -- (5.5,0)
    node[midway,above]{\small Multiply by $A$};

     \node at (9,4) {\bf After multiplication by $A$};
\draw[->,black] (6,0) -- (12,0);
\draw[->,black] (9,-3) -- (9,3);
\draw[-,gray] (-3,0) -- (3,0);
    \node at (8.65,-0.35) {$\mathbf{0}$};   
    \draw[->,red,thick] (9,0) -- (10.25,2.5) node[above right] {$b$};
\draw[-,gray] (7.5,-3) -- (10.5,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<p>
<alert>Questions.</alert>
</p>

<ul>
<li>
Where does <m>A</m> send <m>0</m>?
Since the transformation is linear,
<m>T(0)=A0=0</m>.
</li>

<li>
What does it mean to solve <m>Ax=b</m>?
It asks whether <m>b</m> lies in the column space of <m>A</m>.
</li>
</ul>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="connections-to-calculus">
<title>Connections to Calculus</title>

<p>
<alert>Linearity reminders (differentiation and integration).</alert>
Consider differentiable functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, and scalars
<m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, then
</p>

<me>
af+bg
</me>

<p> is their linear combination. The differentiation
is linear in <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, in particular,
differentiation satisfies
</p>

<me>
\frac{d}{dx}\bigl(af+bg\bigr)
=
af'+bg'.
</me>

<p>
Similarly, integration is a linear operation:
</p>

<me>
\int (af+bg)\,dx
=
a\int f\,dx
+
b\int g\,dx.
</me>

<p>
These properties are examples of <term>linearity</term>, the same idea
that appears in matrix transformations.
</p>

</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="differentiation-matrix-transformation">
<title>Visual: Differentiation as a Matrix Transformation</title>

<p>
<alert>Explanation.</alert>
We represent a polynomial by its coefficient vector. The derivative is a
linear operator that can be written as matrix multiplication. The diagram
below maps a coefficient vector to the coefficient vector of its derivative.
</p>

<image width="100%">
<shortdescription>Diagram showing the derivative matrix mapping polynomial coefficients to the coefficients of the derivative.</shortdescription>
<latex-image width="100%">
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (-6.5,-1) rectangle (-5.5,1);
  \node at (-6,0) {$\begin{bmatrix} a \\[2pt] b \\[2pt] c \end{bmatrix}$};
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (-4,-1) rectangle (2,1);
  \node at(-1,0) {$\
    \text{Derivative matrix}\;D \;=\;\
    \begin{bmatrix}
      2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\
      0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
    \end{bmatrix}$};
  \draw[thick,fill=blue!8] (4,-1) rectangle (5,1);
  \node at (4.5,0) {$\begin{bmatrix} 2a \\[2pt] b \end{bmatrix}$};

  \draw[->] (-5.5,0) -- node[label, above]{apply $D$} (-4,0);
  \draw[->] (2,0) -- node[label, above]{$D \cdot \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{bmatrix}$} (4,0);\

  \node at (-0.7,-1.6) {For $p(x)=ax^2+bx+c$, we have $p'(x)=2ax+b$. Coefficients map via $D$.};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
<p>
<alert>Example 1 (Quadratic, first derivative).</alert>
</p>

<me>
p(x)=ax^2+bx+c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
p'(x)=2ax+b.
</me>

<p>
In terms of coefficient vectors,
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
2a\\
b
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
The matrix
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
</me>

<p>
represents differentiation on the space of quadratic polynomials.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Example 2 (Quadratic, second derivative).</alert>
</p>

<me>
p''(x)=2a.
</me>

<p>
Using coefficient vectors,
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
2a
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Thus the second-derivative operator is represented by the matrix
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
<alert>Example 3 (Cubic, first derivative).</alert>
</p>

<me>
p(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
p'(x)=3ax^2+2bx+c.
</me>

<p>
The corresponding coefficient transformation is
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c\\
d
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
3a\\
2b\\
c
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
3 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
a\\
b\\
c\\
d
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Therefore, the matrix
</p>

<me>
\begin{bmatrix}
3 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
</me>

<p>
represents differentiation on the space of polynomials of degree at most
three.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Connection.</alert>
Differentiation is a linear transformation because
</p>

<me>
D(ap_1+bp_2)
=
aDp_1+bDp_2.
</me>

<p>
Consequently, differentiation can be represented by a matrix acting on
coefficient vectors. This illustrates an important connection between
calculus operators and matrix transformations on finite-dimensional
</p></subsection>
<subsection xml:id="basic-geometric-transformations">
<title>Basic Geometric Transformations (Matrices from Notes)</title>

<p>
<alert>Shear.</alert>
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 3\\
0 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
A shear transformation slides points horizontally while preserving
vertical position. Straight lines remain straight, but angles and shapes
may be distorted.
</p>

<image width="50%">
  <shortdescription>Shear transformation visual</shortdescription>
  <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray!50] (-1,-1) grid (4,3);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick,red] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (4,1) -- (3,1) -- cycle;
  \node at (2.2,1.2) {\small \color{red}shear};
  \node at (0.5,1.2) {\small \color{blue}original};
    \end{tikzpicture}
  </latex-image>
</image>

<p>
<alert>Contraction.</alert>
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac12 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; \frac12
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
A contraction scales all vectors toward the origin by a factor of
<m>\frac12</m>.
</p>
<image width="50%">
  <shortdescription>Visual for contraction</shortdescription>
  <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray!50] (-1,-1) grid (4,3);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (2,2) -- (0,2) -- cycle;
\draw[thick,red] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
  \node at (0.7,1.2) {\tiny \color{red}contraction};
  \node at (1.5,2.2) {\tiny \color{blue}original};
    \end{tikzpicture}
  </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
<alert>Dilation.</alert>
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 2
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
A dilation scales all vectors away from the origin by a factor of
<m>2</m>.
</p>
<image width="50%">
  <shortdescription>Visual for dilation</shortdescription>
  <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray!50] (-1,-1) grid (4,3);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick,red] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (2,2) -- (0,2) -- cycle;
  \node at (1.5,1.2) {\tiny \color{red}dilation};
  \node at (0.5,0.2) {\tiny \color{blue}original};
    \end{tikzpicture}
  </latex-image>
</image>  

<p>
<alert>Rotation (90°).</alert>
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 &amp; -1\\
1 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
This matrix rotates every vector counterclockwise by
<m>90^\circ</m>.
</p>

<image width="50%">
  <shortdescription>Visual for rotation by 90 degrees</shortdescription>
  <latex-image>
    \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[gray!50] (-1,-1)  grid (4,3);
\draw[gray!50] (-1,3) grid(4,-1);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (2,1) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
\draw[thick,red] (0,0) -- (0,2) -- (-1,2) -- (-1,0) -- cycle;
  \node at (-0.5,0.5) {\tiny \color{red}rotation};
  \node at (0.5,0.5) {\tiny \color{blue}original};
    \end{tikzpicture}
  </latex-image>
</image>  

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="standard-matrix">
<title>The Matrix of a Linear Transformation</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="standard-matrix-objectives">
<title>Objectives</title>

<ul>
    <li>Find the standard matrix corresponding to a given linear transformation.</li>
    <li>Understand onto and one-to-one and recognize them from the matrix.</li>
    <li>Analyze a linear transformation by its standard matrix.</li>
</ul>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="standard-basis">
<title>Standard Vectors (Standard Basis)</title>

<p>
<alert>Definition.</alert>
The <term>standard vectors</term> (or
<term>standard basis vectors</term>) in
<m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> are
</p>

<me>
e_1=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
e_2=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1\\
\vdots\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\dots,
\qquad
e_n=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
1
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Each vector <m>e_j</m> has a <m>1</m> in the
<m>j</m>-th position and <m>0</m> everywhere else.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="coordinate-decomposition">
<title>Coordinate Decomposition</title>

<p>
For a vector
</p>

<me>
x=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2\\
\vdots\\
x_n
\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb{R}^n,
</me>

<p>
we may write
</p>

<me>
x
=
x_1e_1+x_2e_2+\cdots+x_ne_n,
</me>

<p>
where <m>e_1,\dots,e_n</m> are the standard basis vectors.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="standard-matrix-definition">
<title>Standard Matrix</title>

<p>
<alert>Standard matrix (columns are images of the standard basis vectors).</alert>
If
<m>T:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m</m>
is a linear transformation, then there is a unique matrix
<m>A</m> such that
</p>

<me>
T(x)=Ax.
</me>

<p>
The columns of <m>A</m> are the images of the standard basis vectors:
</p>

<me>
A
=
\bigl[
T(e_1)\;
T(e_2)\;
\dots\;
T(e_n)
\bigr].
</me>

<p>
Thus, to determine the standard matrix of a linear transformation,
compute the images of the standard basis vectors and place those image
vectors into the columns of a matrix.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Remark.</alert>
We already know that every matrix transformation is linear.
Combining this fact with the theorem above shows that the set of all
linear transformations from <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> to
<m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> is equivalent to the set of all
<m>m\times n</m> matrices.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<example xml:id="standard-matrix-example">
<title>Finding a Standard Matrix</title>

<p>
Find the standard matrix for
</p>

<me>
T:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2,
\qquad
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
2x_1+x_2\\
3x_2
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Compute the images of the standard basis vectors:
</p>

<me>
T(e_1)
=
T
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2\\
0
\end{bmatrix},
</me>

<me>
T(e_2)
=
T
\left(
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\
1
\end{bmatrix}
\right)
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
3
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Therefore,
</p>

<me>
A
=
\begin{bmatrix}
T(e_1)  &amp;  T(e_2)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2  &amp;  1\\
0  &amp;  3
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

</example>

<subsubsection xml:id="rotation-example">
<title>Example (Rotation by <m>\pi/5</m>)</title>
<image>
<shortdescription>Visual for rotation by pi/5</shortdescription>
<latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}

%---------------- Left figure ----------------%

\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};

\draw[red,->,thick] (0,0) -- (2,0);
\node[right] at (2,0.5) {$e_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}$};

\coordinate (A) at ({2*cos(36)},{2*sin(36)});

\draw[blue,->,thick] (0,0) -- (A);

\draw[dashed] (A) -- ({2*cos(36)},0);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (0,{2*sin(36)});

\node[below] at ({cos(36)},0) {$\cos(\pi/5)$};
\node[left]  at (0,{sin(36)}) {$\sin(\pi/5)$};

\draw (0.5,0) arc (0:36:0.5);
\node at (0.85,0.23) {$\frac{\pi}{5}$};

%---------------- Right figure ----------------%

\draw[->] (5,0) -- (10,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (7,-0.5) -- (7,2.5) node[above] {$y$};

\draw[red,->,thick] (7,0) -- (7,2);

\node[right] at (7,2)
{$e_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}$};

\coordinate (B) at ({7-2*sin(36)},{2*cos(36)});

\draw[blue,->,thick] (7,0) -- (B);

\draw[dashed] (B) -- ({7-2*sin(36)},0);
\draw[dashed] (B) -- (7,{2*cos(36)});

\node[below] at ({7-sin(36)},0) {$-\sin(\pi/5)\quad$};
\node[right] at (7,{cos(36)}) {$\cos(\pi/5)$};

\draw (7,0.55) arc (90:126:0.55);
\node at (6.8,0.8) {$\frac{\pi}{5}$};

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<p>
A counterclockwise rotation by
<m>\theta=\pi/5</m>
in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>
has standard matrix
</p>

<me>
R_\theta
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta  &amp;  -\sin\theta\\
\sin\theta  &amp;  \cos\theta
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Substituting <m>\theta=\pi/5</m> gives
</p>

<me>
R_{\pi/5}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos(\pi/5)  &amp;  -\sin(\pi/5)\\
\sin(\pi/5)  &amp;  \cos(\pi/5)
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="onto-and-one-to-one">
<title>Onto and One-to-One</title>

<ul>

<li>
<alert>Onto.</alert>
A transformation is onto if every
<m>b\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>
is equal to <m>T(x)</m> for some
<m>x\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
</li>

<li>
<alert>One-to-one.</alert>
A transformation is one-to-one if
<m>T(x)=T(y)</m>
implies <m>x=y</m>.
</li>

</ul>

<p>
<alert>Matrix criteria.</alert>
If <m>A</m> is the standard matrix of
<m>T</m>, then
</p>

<me>
T\text{ is onto}
\iff
\text{columns of }A\text{ span }\mathbb{R}^m
\iff
A\text{ has a pivot in every row}.
</me>

<me>
T\text{ is one-to-one}
\iff
\text{columns of }A\text{ are linearly independent}
\iff
A\text{ has a pivot in every column}.
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="onto-one-to-one-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<ul>

<li>

<p>
Consider
<m>T:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</m>,
defined by
<m>x\mapsto 2x</m>.
Its standard matrix is
</p>

<me>
[2].
</me>

<p>
Since the matrix has a pivot in its only row and column,
the transformation is both onto and one-to-one.
</p>

</li>

<li>

<p>
Consider
</p>

<me>
T:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2,
\qquad
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Its standard matrix is
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp; 0\\
0 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
This transformation is not onto because its range is only the
<m>x</m>-axis in the plane. It is also not one-to-one because
the second column is the zero vector.
</p>

</li>

</ul>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="onto-and-one-to-1">
<title>Onto and One-to-One</title>

<p>
<alert>Onto.</alert>
A linear transformation
<m>T:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m</m>
is called <term>onto</term> if every vector in the codomain
<m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> is the image of some vector in the domain.
That is, for every
<m>b\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>,
there exists an
<m>x\in\mathbb{R}^n</m>
such that
</p>

<me>
T(x)=b.
</me>

<p>
<alert>One-to-One.</alert>
A linear transformation is called
<term>one-to-one</term> if different vectors in the domain are always
mapped to different vectors in the codomain. Equivalently,
</p>

<me>
T(x)=T(y)
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad
x=y.
</me>

<p>
For matrix transformations, onto and one-to-one can be determined by
examining the pivots of the standard matrix.
</p>

<p>
<alert>Matrix Criteria.</alert>
Let <m>A</m> be the standard matrix of a linear transformation
<m>T</m>.
</p>

<me>
T \text{ is onto}
\iff
\text{the columns of }A\text{ span }\mathbb{R}^m
\iff
A\text{ has a pivot in every row}.
</me>

<me>
T \text{ is one-to-one}
\iff
\text{the columns of }A\text{ are linearly independent}
\iff
A\text{ has a pivot in every column}.
</me>

<example xml:id="onto-one-to-one-example-1">
<title>A Transformation that is Onto and One-to-One</title>

<p>
Consider the transformation
</p>

<me>
T:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R},
\qquad
T(x)=2x.
</me>

<p>
Its standard matrix is
</p>

<me>
A=[2].
</me>

<p>
The matrix has a pivot in its only row and its only column.
Therefore, the transformation is both onto and one-to-one.
</p>

</example>

<example xml:id="onto-one-to-one-example-2">
<title>A Transformation that is Neither Onto nor One-to-One</title>

<p>
Consider the transformation
</p>

<me>
T:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2,
\qquad
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}
\mapsto
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
Its standard matrix is
</p>

<me>
A=
\begin{bmatrix}
1  &amp;  0\\
0  &amp;  0
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

<p>
The second row contains no pivot, so the columns do not span
<m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>. Therefore, the transformation is not onto.
</p>

<p>
The second column is the zero vector, so the columns are not linearly
independent. Therefore, the transformation is not one-to-one.
</p>

<p>
Geometrically, every vector in the plane is projected onto the
<m>x</m>-axis. The range is the set
</p>

<me>
\left\{
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
0
\end{bmatrix}
:
x\in\mathbb{R}
\right\}.
</me>

<p>
Since the range is only the <m>x</m>-axis rather than all of
<m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, the transformation is not onto.
Many different vectors have the same image, so it is not one-to-one.
</p>

</example>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="composition">
<title>Composition of Linear Transformations</title>

<p>
Suppose
<m>T:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m</m>
and
<m>S:\mathbb{R}^m\to\mathbb{R}^p</m>
are linear transformations with standard matrices
<m>A</m>
and
<m>B</m>, respectively.
</p>

<p>
If
</p>

<me>
T(x)=Ax
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
S(y)=By,
</me>

<p>
then the composition of the transformations is obtained by applying
<m>T</m> first and then <m>S</m>.
</p>

<me>
(S\circ T)(x)
=
S(T(x)).
</me>

<p>
Substituting <m>T(x)=Ax</m> gives
</p>

<me>
(S\circ T)(x)
=
S(Ax)
=
B(Ax).
</me>

<p>
Using associativity of matrix multiplication,
</p>

<me>
(S\circ T)(x)
=
(BA)x.
</me>

<p>
Therefore, the standard matrix of the composition
<m>S\circ T</m>
is the product
<m>BA</m>.
</p>

<p>
The diagram illustrates the path
</p>
<image>
<shortdescription>Diagram showing the composition of two linear transformations.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

% Boxes
\draw[rounded corners] (0,0) rectangle (2,1);
\draw[rounded corners] (4,0) rectangle (6,1);
\draw[rounded corners] (8,0) rectangle (10,1);

% Points and labels
\fill (0.8,0.5) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (0.9,0.5) {$x$};

\fill (5,0.6) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (5.1,0.6) {$y$};

\fill (9,0.4) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (9.1,0.4) {$z$};

% Lower labels
\node at (3,-0.6) {$y=T(x)=Ax$};
\node at (7,-0.6) {$z=S(y)=By$};

% Horizontal arrows
\draw[blue,->,thick] (0.9,0.5) -- (4,0.5);
\node[above] at (3,0.5) {$T$};

\draw[blue,->,thick] (5.2,0.56) -- (8.9,0.5);
\node[above] at (7,0.5) {$S$};

% Curved composition arrow
\draw[blue,->,thick]
  (1,1.05)
  .. controls (3,2.1) and (7,2.1) ..
  (9,1.05);

\node[above,blue] at (5,2.0) {$S\circ T$};

\end{tikzpicture}

</latex-image>
</image>
<me>
x
\xrightarrow{\;T\;}
y=T(x)
\xrightarrow{\;S\;}
z=S(y).
</me>

<p>
Applying the two transformations in succession is equivalent to applying
a single transformation whose matrix is the product
<m>BA</m>.
</p>

<p>
Thus,
</p>

<me>
S\circ T
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
BA.
</me>

<p>
This provides a geometric interpretation of matrix multiplication:
multiplying matrices corresponds to composing linear transformations.
</p>

</subsection>
</section>